              Set the number of self-recurrence iterations <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> based on the early step emphasis 
</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
              
              
              <span id="alg1.l4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1,\cdots,K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1,\cdots,K</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>



</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
              
              
               Compute <math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\boldsymbol{z}}_{t,0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\boldsymbol{z}}_{t,0}</annotation></semantics></math> using Eq. (<a href="#S3.E1" title="In III-A Overview of Our Decoder ‣ III Our Guided-diffusion-model-based Decoder for Feature Space Analysis ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>)

</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
              
              
               Generate <math id="alg1.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="alg1.l6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\boldsymbol{z}}_{t,0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\boldsymbol{z}}_{t,0}</annotation></semantics></math> via the virtual image save

</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">7:</span></span>
              
              
               Compute the feature <math id="alg1.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\left(\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\left(\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="alg1.l7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">8:</span></span>
              
              
               Compute <math id="alg1.l8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l\left(f\left(\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}\right),\boldsymbol{f}_{s}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l\left(f\left(\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}\right),\boldsymbol{f}_{s}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> using Eq. (<a href="#S3.E2" title="In III-A Overview of Our Decoder ‣ III Our Guided-diffusion-model-based Decoder for Feature Space Analysis ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) and its gradient <math id="alg1.l8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\boldsymbol{z}_{t}}l\left(f\left(\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}\right),\boldsymbol{f}_{s}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\boldsymbol{z}_{t}}l\left(f\left(\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}\right),\boldsymbol{f}_{s}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l9.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">9:</span></span>
              
              
               Normalise and clip <math id="alg1.l9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\boldsymbol{z}_{t}}l\left(f\left(\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}\right),\boldsymbol{f}_{s}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\boldsymbol{z}_{t}}l\left(f\left(\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}\right),\boldsymbol{f}_{s}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l10.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">10:</span></span>
              
              
               Compute <math id="alg1.l10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\theta}^{\prime}\left(\boldsymbol{z}_{t},t\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\theta}^{\prime}\left(\boldsymbol{z}_{t},t\right)</annotation></semantics></math> using Eq. (<a href="#S3.E3" title="In III-A Overview of Our Decoder ‣ III Our Guided-diffusion-model-based Decoder for Feature Space Analysis ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>)

</div>
<div id="alg1.l11" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l11.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">11:</span></span>
              
              
               Sample <math id="alg1.l11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{z}_{t-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{z}_{t-1}</annotation></semantics></math> using Eq. (<a href="#S3.E4" title="In III-A Overview of Our Decoder ‣ III Our Guided-diffusion-model-based Decoder for Feature Space Analysis ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>)

</div>
<div id="alg1.l12" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l12.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">12:</span></span>
              
              
               <span id="alg1.l12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span> <math id="alg1.l12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k&lt;K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k&lt;K</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l12.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">then</span>

 
</div>
<div id="alg1.l13" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l13.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">13:</span></span>
              
              
                Resample <math id="alg1.l13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{z}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{z}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="alg1.l13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{z}_{t-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{z}_{t-1}</annotation></semantics></math> using Eq. (<a href="#S3.E5" title="In III-B Details of Our Decoder ‣ III Our Guided-diffusion-model-based Decoder for Feature Space Analysis ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) 
</div>
<div id="alg1.l14" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l14.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">14:</span></span>
              
              
               <span id="alg1.l14.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l14.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l15" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l15.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">15:</span></span>
              
              
              <span id="alg1.l15.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l15.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l16" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l16.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">16:</span></span>
              
              
             <span id="alg1.l16.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l16.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l17" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l17.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">17:</span></span>
              
              
             Generate <math id="alg1.l17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="alg1.l17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{z}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{z}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> by stable diffusion’s decoder

</div>
<div id="alg1.l18" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l18.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">18:</span></span>
              
              
             <span id="alg1.l18.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span>  <math id="alg1.l18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
</div>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ III-B Details of Our Decoder ‣ III Our Guided-diffusion-model-based Decoder for Feature Space Analysis ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows a flow of operations in our decoder <span id="footnote4" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">4</span>
              
              
              
            Although our decoder is based on the guided diffusion model proposed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>, <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>, preliminary experiments let us exclude one of its main modules, called backward universal guidance. The reason is that this module directly optimises pixels in an image without considering the progress of the reverse process, which actually offers little reduction of the loss function and often produces very unrealistic or corrupted images.</span></span></span>. The main operations at lines <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math>-<math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>11</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">11</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="17" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>17</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">17</annotation></semantics></math> have been already described in the previous subsection. The following part details the other operations such as early step emphasis at line <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>, self-recurrence at lines <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>12</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="13" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>13</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">13</annotation></semantics></math>, virtual image save at line <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6</annotation></semantics></math>, and gradient normalisation and clipping at line <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>9</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Self-recurrence is motivated by the fact that <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> reverse steps (<math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=1000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=1000</annotation></semantics></math> in our case) are insufficient to generate an image whose feature is within acceptable proximity to <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{f}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{f}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>. With respect to this, increasing <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is difficult because the diffusion model in our decoder is pre-trained with this <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>. To overcome this, self-recurrence <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>, <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>, <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>, <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite> is employed to resample <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{z}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{z}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{z}_{t-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{z}_{t-1}</annotation></semantics></math> by following the standard forward step below:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\boldsymbol{z}_{t}=\sqrt{\alpha_{t}}\boldsymbol{z}_{t-1}+\sqrt{1-\alpha_{t}}\boldsymbol{\Delta}\;\;\;\;\mbox{where }\boldsymbol{\Delta}\sim\mathcal{N}\left(\boldsymbol{0},\mbox{{I}}\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msqrt><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝚫</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1.12em;" width="1.12em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mtext>where </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝚫</mi></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">I</mtext><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\boldsymbol{z}_{t}=\sqrt{\alpha_{t}}\boldsymbol{z}_{t-1}+\sqrt{1-\alpha_{t}}\boldsymbol{\Delta}\;\;\;\;\mbox{where }\boldsymbol{\Delta}\sim\mathcal{N}\left(\boldsymbol{0},\mbox{{I}}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Since this resampled <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{z}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{z}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> preserves the information in <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{z}_{t-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{z}_{t-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, it is likely to yield an image whose feature is closer to <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{f}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{f}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> than <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{z}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{z}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> before self-recurrence. This way, self-recurrence iteratively refines <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{z}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{z}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> by incorporating the information from the subsequent <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t-1)</annotation></semantics></math>th step while maintaining the noise scale at the current <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>th step.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The overall object layout of a generated image is primarily established during early reverse steps <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>. Thus, our decoder’s image generation fails if this layout is inadequate. To address this, we design an early step emphasis strategy that prioritises early reverse steps. Specifically, for the first <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> reverse steps (i.e., <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\geq T-T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\geq T-T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> in Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ III-B Details of Our Decoder ‣ III Our Guided-diffusion-model-based Decoder for Feature Space Analysis ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) the number of self-recurrence iterations <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> is set to a large value while using a much smaller <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> at the remaining steps. In our case, <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> is set to <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1000</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8</annotation></semantics></math> at the first <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}=5</annotation></semantics></math> reverse steps and the others, respectively. Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Effect of the Early Step Emphasis Strategy ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a> provides an ablation study to empirically show the effectiveness of the early step emphasis strategy.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.07936v1/figure_grad_dist2.png" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/147;" width="476" height="147" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 2: </span>Distributions of values in <math id="S3.F2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\boldsymbol{z}_{t}}l\left(f\left(\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}\right),\boldsymbol{f}_{s}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\boldsymbol{z}_{t}}l\left(f\left(\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}\right),\boldsymbol{f}_{s}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> obtained at the first self-recurrence (i.e., <math id="S3.F2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=0</annotation></semantics></math>) iteration of the first seven reverse steps (i.e., <math id="S3.F2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=999" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>999</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=999</annotation></semantics></math>-<math id="S3.F2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="993" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>993</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">993</annotation></semantics></math>th steps) when the target feature extractor <math id="S3.F2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as CLIP’s image encoder (a), ResNet-50 (b) and ViT-H/14 (c). Here, <math id="S3.F2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{f}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{f}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> is specified as the feature extracted from the top-left actual image in Fig. <a href="#S4.F3" title="Fig. 3 ‣ IV-A Results Targeting CLIP’s Image Encoder ‣ IV Experimental Results ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> (please see Section <a href="#S4" title="IV Experimental Results ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV</span></a> for more details). In each visualised distribution, the dotted indicates the mean and the solid lines represent three times standard deviation for (a) and (b) and twice standard deviation for (c).</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Stable diffusion’s decoder produces an image <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> as a tensor of shape <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\times H\times W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\times H\times W</annotation></semantics></math> (in our case <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=3</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H=W=512" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>512</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H=W=512</annotation></semantics></math>) where pixels can take both negative and positive values. The ideal way to precisely compute <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\boldsymbol{z}_{t}}l\left(f\left(\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}\right),\boldsymbol{f}_{s}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\boldsymbol{z}_{t}}l\left(f\left(\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}\right),\boldsymbol{f}_{s}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> is to scale values in <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and save <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> into an actual image file, which is then loaded and encoded into <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\left(\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\left(\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> through <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>. However, this is infeasible because the computation graph is broken when <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is saved into an external image file. Thus, to simulate image save on a GPU’s VRAM, a virtual image save module is implemented so that <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is scaled and rounded to make pixels take discrete values between <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="255" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>255</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">255</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="footnote5" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">5</span>
              
              
              
            Due to the small difference in image rescaling, <math id="footnote5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\left(\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\left(\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> obtained via the visual image save differs marginally from <math id="footnote5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\left(\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\left(\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> obtained by loading an actual image file. In the case where <math id="footnote5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as CLIP’s image encoder to produce <math id="footnote5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1024" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1024</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1024</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional feature vectors, their squared Euclidean distance is about <math id="footnote5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.011" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.011</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.011</annotation></semantics></math>, resulting in only minor semantic variations in generated images.</span></span></span>. Here, straight through estimator <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite> is used to approximate the backpropagation over the round function of <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> as its identify function.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">One crucial issue to stabilise our decoder’s image generation is how to balance <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\theta}\left(\boldsymbol{z}_{t},t\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\theta}\left(\boldsymbol{z}_{t},t\right)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\boldsymbol{z}_{t}}l\left(f\left(\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}\right),\boldsymbol{f}_{s}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\boldsymbol{z}_{t}}l\left(f\left(\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}\right),\boldsymbol{f}_{s}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> to compute <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\theta}^{\prime}\left(\boldsymbol{z}_{t},t\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\theta}^{\prime}\left(\boldsymbol{z}_{t},t\right)</annotation></semantics></math> in Eq. (<a href="#S3.E3" title="In III-A Overview of Our Decoder ‣ III Our Guided-diffusion-model-based Decoder for Feature Space Analysis ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>). Another key issue is that exceptionally large values in <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\boldsymbol{z}_{t}}l\left(f\left(\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}\right),\boldsymbol{f}_{s}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\boldsymbol{z}_{t}}l\left(f\left(\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}\right),\boldsymbol{f}_{s}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> excessively distort the originally predicted values in <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\theta}\left(\boldsymbol{z}_{t},t\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\theta}\left(\boldsymbol{z}_{t},t\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, leading to the generation of corrupted images. For the first issue, <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\boldsymbol{z}_{t}}l\left(f\left(\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}\right),\boldsymbol{f}_{s}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\boldsymbol{z}_{t}}l\left(f\left(\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}\right),\boldsymbol{f}_{s}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> is normalised by multiplying <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{||\epsilon_{\theta}\left(\boldsymbol{z}_{t},t\right)||_{2}}{||\nabla_{\boldsymbol{z}_{t}}l\left(f\left(\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}\right),\boldsymbol{f}_{s}\right)||_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{||\epsilon_{\theta}\left(\boldsymbol{z}_{t},t\right)||_{2}}{||\nabla_{\boldsymbol{z}_{t}}l\left(f\left(\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}\right),\boldsymbol{f}_{s}\right)||_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="||\boldsymbol{a}||_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝒂</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">||\boldsymbol{a}||_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is the L2 norm computed by flatting the tensor <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒂</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{a}</annotation></semantics></math> into a vector. The normalised <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\boldsymbol{z}_{t}}l\left(f\left(\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}\right),\boldsymbol{f}_{s}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\boldsymbol{z}_{t}}l\left(f\left(\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}\right),\boldsymbol{f}_{s}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> has the same contribution as <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\theta}\left(\boldsymbol{z}_{t},t\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\theta}\left(\boldsymbol{z}_{t},t\right)</annotation></semantics></math> in terms of their L2 norms. For the second issue, we found that values in <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\boldsymbol{z}_{t}}l\left(f\left(\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}\right),\boldsymbol{f}_{s}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\boldsymbol{z}_{t}}l\left(f\left(\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}\right),\boldsymbol{f}_{s}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> approximately follow a Gaussian distribution whose mean is nearly zero as demonstrated in Fig. <a href="#S3.F2" title="Fig. 2 ‣ III-B Details of Our Decoder ‣ III Our Guided-diffusion-model-based Decoder for Feature Space Analysis ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. Based on this, gradient clipping is designed such that if values in <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\boldsymbol{z}_{t}}l\left(f\left(\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}\right),\boldsymbol{f}_{s}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\boldsymbol{z}_{t}}l\left(f\left(\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}\right),\boldsymbol{f}_{s}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> fall outside the range <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm\nabla_{thres}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">±</mo><msub><mo>∇</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm\nabla_{thres}</annotation></semantics></math>, they are clipped to their nearest boundary, either <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{thres}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mo>∇</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{thres}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\nabla_{thres}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><msub><mo>∇</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\nabla_{thres}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In particular, as depicted by solid lines in Fig. <a href="#S3.F2" title="Fig. 2 ‣ III-B Details of Our Decoder ‣ III Our Guided-diffusion-model-based Decoder for Feature Space Analysis ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{thres}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mo>∇</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{thres}</annotation></semantics></math> is set to three times the standard deviation of values in <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\boldsymbol{z}_{t}}l\left(f\left(\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}\right),\boldsymbol{f}_{s}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\boldsymbol{z}_{t}}l\left(f\left(\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}\right),\boldsymbol{f}_{s}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is CLIP’s image encoder or ResNet-50. When using ViT-H/14 as <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{thres}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mo>∇</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{thres}</annotation></semantics></math> is set to twice the standard deviation due to the broad spread of values in <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\boldsymbol{z}_{t}}l\left(f\left(\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}\right),\boldsymbol{f}_{s}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\boldsymbol{z}_{t}}l\left(f\left(\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}\right),\boldsymbol{f}_{s}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> as shown in Fig. <a href="#S3.F2" title="Fig. 2 ‣ III-B Details of Our Decoder ‣ III Our Guided-diffusion-model-based Decoder for Feature Space Analysis ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> (c). Finally, <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\boldsymbol{z}_{t}}l\left(f\left(\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}\right),\boldsymbol{f}_{s}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\boldsymbol{z}_{t}}l\left(f\left(\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}\right),\boldsymbol{f}_{s}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> fixed by the above-mentioned gradient normalisation and clipping is weighted by <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> to compute <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\theta}^{\prime}\left(\boldsymbol{z}_{t},t\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\theta}^{\prime}\left(\boldsymbol{z}_{t},t\right)</annotation></semantics></math> in Eq. (<a href="#S3.E3" title="In III-A Overview of Our Decoder ‣ III Our Guided-diffusion-model-based Decoder for Feature Space Analysis ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>). All the experiments in the next section are done by fixing <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>. Please see Appendixes <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Effect of 𝑤_𝑔 ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a> and <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Effect of ∇_{𝑡⁢ℎ⁢𝑟⁢𝑒⁢𝑠} ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a> for results obtained by different values of <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{thres}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mo>∇</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{thres}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">IV </span><span id="S4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Experimental Results</span></h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We aim to evaluate our decoder from two perspectives: The first is the evaluation as a decoder, specifically whether our decoder can successfully generate images whose features closely match a user-specified feature <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{f}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{f}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>. The second perspective is the evaluation as a feature space analyser, namely whether generated images provide meaningful visual cues about the feature space of the target feature extractor <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>. One key issue in designing experiments is determining how to define <math id="S4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{f}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{f}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> that serves as the goal—that is, the feature of a generated image should be as similar to <math id="S4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{f}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{f}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> as possible. We define <math id="S4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{f}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{f}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> as the feature that is extracted from an actual image via <math id="S4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>. This also facilitates the evaluation of the second perspective because we can interpret the adequacy of visual contents in a generated image by referring to the actual image.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The experiments below target three feature extractors to evaluate the generality of our decoder. CLIP’s image encoder with ResNet-50 backbone <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> is selected as the first feature extractor because of its popularity and stability. Then, ResNet-50 is used as the second feature extractor to perform a comparative study against an existing decoder based on a conditional diffusion model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>. Last, Vision Transformer (ViT) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> is used as the third feature extractor to compare its feature space with that of less accurate ResNet-50.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">By setting <math id="S4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> to each of the three feature extractors described above, <math id="S4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{f}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{f}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> is extracted from every one of the <math id="S4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>15</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">15</annotation></semantics></math> actual images shown in the leftmost column in Figs. <a href="#S4.F3" title="Fig. 3 ‣ IV-A Results Targeting CLIP’s Image Encoder ‣ IV Experimental Results ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and <a href="#S4.F4" title="Fig. 4 ‣ IV-A Results Targeting CLIP’s Image Encoder ‣ IV Experimental Results ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. These images are selected from the dataset used in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>, <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite> (the upper three images in Fig. <a href="#S4.F3" title="Fig. 3 ‣ IV-A Results Targeting CLIP’s Image Encoder ‣ IV Experimental Results ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), MSCOCO dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite> (the five images from the bottom of Fig. <a href="#S4.F3" title="Fig. 3 ‣ IV-A Results Targeting CLIP’s Image Encoder ‣ IV Experimental Results ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and the uppermost image in Fig. <a href="#S4.F4" title="Fig. 4 ‣ IV-A Results Targeting CLIP’s Image Encoder ‣ IV Experimental Results ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>), and ImageNet dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite> (the six images except the uppermost one in Fig. <a href="#S4.F4" title="Fig. 4 ‣ IV-A Results Targeting CLIP’s Image Encoder ‣ IV Experimental Results ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>). The reason for the small number of image generation attempts is our decoder’s computation time as discussed in Section <a href="#S4.SS4" title="IV-D Discussions ‣ IV Experimental Results ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV-D</span></a>. Another reason is the limitation of computational resources, as all the experiments were conducted using only one NVIDIA GeForce RTX 3090 with <math id="S4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="24" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>24</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">24</annotation></semantics></math>GB VRAM and one NVIDIA RTX 4000 Ada with <math id="S4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>20</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20</annotation></semantics></math>GB VRAM. However, as seen from the leftmost column in Figs. <a href="#S4.F3" title="Fig. 3 ‣ IV-A Results Targeting CLIP’s Image Encoder ‣ IV Experimental Results ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and <a href="#S4.F4" title="Fig. 4 ‣ IV-A Results Targeting CLIP’s Image Encoder ‣ IV Experimental Results ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, the <math id="S4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>15</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">15</annotation></semantics></math> images encompass a variety of objects (e.g., person, animal, vehicle and food) presented with different backgrounds, so we believe that they are enough for evaluating the overall performance of our decoder.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The hyper-parameters specific to our decoder (<math id="S4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>) remain fixed across all the experiments, except for <math id="S4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{thres}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mo>∇</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{thres}</annotation></semantics></math> that is set differently when <math id="S4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is ViT or the others. Of course, better results might be obtained if finer hyper-parameter tuning were conducted for each experiment. Please see Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="III-B Details of Our Decoder ‣ III Our Guided-diffusion-model-based Decoder for Feature Space Analysis ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III-B</span></a> for the exact hyper-parameter values. The configuration of the stable diffusion in our decoder is the same as the one in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>, <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">IV-A</span> </span><span id="S4.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Results Targeting CLIP’s Image Encoder</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figs. <a href="#S4.F3" title="Fig. 3 ‣ IV-A Results Targeting CLIP’s Image Encoder ‣ IV Experimental Results ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and <a href="#S4.F4" title="Fig. 4 ‣ IV-A Results Targeting CLIP’s Image Encoder ‣ IV Experimental Results ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> show images generated by our decoder when using CLIP’s image encoder with ResNet-50 backbone as the target feature extractor. For each row, <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{f}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{f}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> is extracted as the feature of the leftmost actual image and our decoder’s task is to generate an image whose feature is as similar to <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{f}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{f}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> as possible in terms of their squared Euclidean distance. Considering the randomness in generated images, our decoder is run three times. For each run, the following two images are presented: The first is the “finally generated image” that is obtained at the end of the image generation process, and the second is the “best image” whose feature yields the shortest squared Euclidean distance to <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{f}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{f}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> during the image generation process. The number under each generated image shows the squared Euclidean distance between its feature and <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{f}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{f}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.07936v1/figure_gen_result1_s.png" id="S4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_portrait" style="aspect-ratio:429/597;" width="429" height="597" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 3: </span>Images generated by our decoder when using CLIP’s image encoder with ResNet-50 backbone as the target feature extractor. Each row presents three runs of our decoder’s image generation to generate an image whose feature is similar to the one extracted from the leftmost image. For each run, the finally generated image and the best image are shown together with the squared Euclidean distances between their features and the one of the leftmost image.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.07936v1/figure_gen_result2_s.png" id="S4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_portrait" style="aspect-ratio:429/536;" width="429" height="536" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 4: </span>(Continued from Fig. <a href="#S4.F3" title="Fig. 3 ‣ IV-A Results Targeting CLIP’s Image Encoder ‣ IV Experimental Results ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) Images generated by our decoder when using CLIP’s image encoder with ResNet-50 backbone as the target feature extractor.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">First of all, the comparison between the finally generated image and the best one in each run indicates that both of them are visually very similar. However, enriching the best image with detailed contents to produce the finally generated one degrades its squared Euclidean distance and visual similarity to the actual image. Thus, the following discussion focuses only on the best image of each run.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">As seen from Figs. <a href="#S4.F3" title="Fig. 3 ‣ IV-A Results Targeting CLIP’s Image Encoder ‣ IV Experimental Results ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and <a href="#S4.F4" title="Fig. 4 ‣ IV-A Results Targeting CLIP’s Image Encoder ‣ IV Experimental Results ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, our decoder successfully generates images that have not only very similar features to <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{f}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{f}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> but also similar visual appearances to the actual image from which <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{f}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{f}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> is extracted. Quantitatively, the average pairwise squared Euclidean distance among the <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>15</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">15</annotation></semantics></math> actual images in Figs. <a href="#S4.F3" title="Fig. 3 ‣ IV-A Results Targeting CLIP’s Image Encoder ‣ IV Experimental Results ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and <a href="#S4.F4" title="Fig. 4 ‣ IV-A Results Targeting CLIP’s Image Encoder ‣ IV Experimental Results ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> is <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6.76881" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6.76881</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6.76881</annotation></semantics></math> and the one among <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math> images that are carefully chosen from the tusker category of ImageNet dataset and confirmed to have similar visual perception by human is <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.40302" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1.40302</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.40302</annotation></semantics></math> (Please see Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A 10 Carefully-chosen Tusker Images ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> for these <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math> images). In comparison, the squared Euclidean distance between a best image and its corresponding actual image ranges from <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.02269" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.02269</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.02269</annotation></semantics></math> (run 2 in the bottom row of Fig. <a href="#S4.F3" title="Fig. 3 ‣ IV-A Results Targeting CLIP’s Image Encoder ‣ IV Experimental Results ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) to <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.14704" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.14704</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.14704</annotation></semantics></math> (run 1 in the second row from the bottom of Fig. <a href="#S4.F4" title="Fig. 4 ‣ IV-A Results Targeting CLIP’s Image Encoder ‣ IV Experimental Results ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>). This validates our decoder’s ability to generate images each of which has an exceptionally similar feature to <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{f}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{f}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Note that our objective is not to generate realistic images, but rather images whose features closely match <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{f}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{f}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>. This enables us to analyse the feature space of the target feature extractor, more specifically, to investigate what images are located in proximity to the actual image from which <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{f}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{f}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> is extracted. Taking the first row in Fig <a href="#S4.F3" title="Fig. 3 ‣ IV-A Results Targeting CLIP’s Image Encoder ‣ IV Experimental Results ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> as an example, each of the generated images—like the actual image—appears to show the same type of dog with a green object around its neck, sitting outdoors with trees in the background (please enlarge the image to see details). But, the dog’s legs and tail are very unnatural, suggesting that CLIP’s image encoder pays little attention to their anatomical topology. Also, the second row in Fig. <a href="#S4.F3" title="Fig. 3 ‣ IV-A Results Targeting CLIP’s Image Encoder ‣ IV Experimental Results ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> implies that the feature extracted from the actual image by CLIP’s image encoder only captures a woman with red hair in a visually complex background. Please check the other rows in the same logic. This way, our decoder can offer insights in how image features are extracted by a target feature extractor (CLIP’s image encoder in this experiment) and we believe that these insights are valuable for improving it.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Also, Figs. <a href="#S4.F3" title="Fig. 3 ‣ IV-A Results Targeting CLIP’s Image Encoder ‣ IV Experimental Results ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and <a href="#S4.F4" title="Fig. 4 ‣ IV-A Results Targeting CLIP’s Image Encoder ‣ IV Experimental Results ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> demonstrate that the variance among the best images is relatively low. This means that most of the best images closely match the corresponding actual images in terms of their squared Euclidean distances as well as their visual similarities. Although squared Euclidean distances are occasionally unstable as shown in the second row from the bottom of Fig. <a href="#S4.F3" title="Fig. 3 ‣ IV-A Results Targeting CLIP’s Image Encoder ‣ IV Experimental Results ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and the third row from the top of Fig. <a href="#S4.F4" title="Fig. 4 ‣ IV-A Results Targeting CLIP’s Image Encoder ‣ IV Experimental Results ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, all the best images successfully capture the main contents in the corresponding actual images.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">IV-B</span> </span><span id="S4.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Comparative Results Targeting ResNet-50</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The main purpose of this experiment is to compare our decoder to Representation Conditional Diffusion Model (RCDM) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>. RCDM is trained by conditioning its image generation on <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{f}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{f}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> so that a generated image is similar to the actual image from which <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{f}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{f}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> is extracted. In contrast, our decoder requires no training, instead guides the image generation of a pre-trained diffusion model (stable diffusion in this paper). Thus, our aim is to examine which of RCDM or our decoder can generate an image whose feature matches <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{f}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{f}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> more closely.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Figs. <a href="#S4.F5" title="Fig. 5 ‣ IV-B Comparative Results Targeting ResNet-50 ‣ IV Experimental Results ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> and <a href="#S4.F6" title="Fig. 6 ‣ IV-B Comparative Results Targeting ResNet-50 ‣ IV Experimental Results ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> present images generated by our decoder and RCDM. To be precise, ResNet-50 configured with ResNet50_Weights.IMAGENET1K_V1 <span id="footnote6" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">6</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">6</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">6</span>
              
              
              
            <a href="https://docs.pytorch.org/vision/main/models/generated/torchvision.models.resnet50.html#torchvision.models.ResNet50_Weights" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://docs.pytorch.org/vision/main/models/generated/torchvision.models.resnet50.html#torchvision.models.ResNet50_Weights</a></span></span></span> is used as the target feature extractor. For each row, <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{f}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{f}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as the output of the penultimate layer of this ResNet-50 for the leftmost actual image. RCDM used here is actually trained using a large number of images in ImageNet dataset and their features defined in this manner. As in the previous section, our decoder is run three times and the best image for each run is shown. For RCDM, three images through the conditional generation by <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{f}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{f}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> are displayed. The number under each generated image indicates the squared Euclidean distance between its feature and <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{f}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{f}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F5" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.07936v1/figure_gen_result_res1_s.png" id="S4.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_portrait" style="aspect-ratio:429/589;" width="429" height="589" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 5: </span>A comparison between images generated by our decoder and the ones by RCDM when using ResNet-50 as the target feature extractor. For each row, the image generation of our decoder and the one of RCDM are respectively guided and conditioned through the feature extracted from the leftmost actual image. The best images from three respective runs are shown for our decoder while three images generated via the conditional generation are displayed for RCDM. The number under each generated image indicates the squared Euclidean distance between its feature and the one of the corresponding actual image.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.F6" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.07936v1/figure_gen_result_res2_s.png" id="S4.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:429/528;" width="429" height="528" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 6: </span>(Continued from Fig. <a href="#S4.F5" title="Fig. 5 ‣ IV-B Comparative Results Targeting ResNet-50 ‣ IV Experimental Results ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) A comparison between images generated by our decoder and the ones by RCDM when using ResNet-50 as the target feature extractor.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Figs. <a href="#S4.F5" title="Fig. 5 ‣ IV-B Comparative Results Targeting ResNet-50 ‣ IV Experimental Results ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> and <a href="#S4.F6" title="Fig. 6 ‣ IV-B Comparative Results Targeting ResNet-50 ‣ IV Experimental Results ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> demonstrate that our decoder generates images whose features are much closer to <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{f}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{f}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> than RCDM. Specifically, most of squared Euclidean distances for images generated by our decoder are single-digit while those for RCDM are three-digit. Furthermore, with respect to ResNet-50 features, the average pairwise squared Euclidean distance among the <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>15</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">15</annotation></semantics></math> actual images in Figs. <a href="#S4.F5" title="Fig. 5 ‣ IV-B Comparative Results Targeting ResNet-50 ‣ IV Experimental Results ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> and <a href="#S4.F6" title="Fig. 6 ‣ IV-B Comparative Results Targeting ResNet-50 ‣ IV Experimental Results ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> is <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="715.830" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>715.830</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">715.830</annotation></semantics></math> and the one among the <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math> tusker images carefully chosen from ImageNet dataset is <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="171.156" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>171.156</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">171.156</annotation></semantics></math> (Please see Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A 10 Carefully-chosen Tusker Images ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> for these images). Based on this, when we make a rough criterion that two images whose squared Euclidean distance is less than <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="200" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>200</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">200</annotation></semantics></math> are perceptually similar, <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="22" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>22</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">22</annotation></semantics></math> of the <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="45" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>45</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">45</annotation></semantics></math> images generated by RCDM in Figs. <a href="#S4.F5" title="Fig. 5 ‣ IV-B Comparative Results Targeting ResNet-50 ‣ IV Experimental Results ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> and <a href="#S4.F6" title="Fig. 6 ‣ IV-B Comparative Results Targeting ResNet-50 ‣ IV Experimental Results ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> are regarded as perceptually similar to their corresponding actual images. On the other hand, all the <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="45" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>45</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">45</annotation></semantics></math> images generated by our decoder significantly surpass this criterion. Actually, although RCDM can generate images characterised by similar object layouts to the corresponding actual images, the objects in these images are not the same type. This is illustrated by, for instance, dogs in first row of Fig. <a href="#S4.F5" title="Fig. 5 ‣ IV-B Comparative Results Targeting ResNet-50 ‣ IV Experimental Results ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, birds in the second row of Fig. <a href="#S4.F6" title="Fig. 6 ‣ IV-B Comparative Results Targeting ResNet-50 ‣ IV Experimental Results ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> and foods in the third row from the bottom of Fig. <a href="#S4.F6" title="Fig. 6 ‣ IV-B Comparative Results Targeting ResNet-50 ‣ IV Experimental Results ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Compared to RCDM, our decoder performs more precise generation of images that capture main contents in the corresponding actual images like object type and background, despite the lack of realness. Again it should be noted that our purpose is not to generate realistic images, but images whose features are close to <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{f}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{f}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> in order to analyse the feature space of the target feature extractor (ResNet-50 in this experiment). Conversely, the unrealness of generated images rather implies the imperfectness of the feature space. In addition, the comparison of Figs. <a href="#S4.F3" title="Fig. 3 ‣ IV-A Results Targeting CLIP’s Image Encoder ‣ IV Experimental Results ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>–<a href="#S4.F6" title="Fig. 6 ‣ IV-B Comparative Results Targeting ResNet-50 ‣ IV Experimental Results ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> suggests that the quality of images generated by targeting CLIP’s image encoder seems higher than the one of images generated by targeting ResNet-50. For example, the dogs in the generated images in the top row of Fig. <a href="#S4.F5" title="Fig. 5 ‣ IV-B Comparative Results Targeting ResNet-50 ‣ IV Experimental Results ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> lack green objects around their necks, the cars in the generated images in the third row of Fig. <a href="#S4.F6" title="Fig. 6 ‣ IV-B Comparative Results Targeting ResNet-50 ‣ IV Experimental Results ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> incorrectly include red that is indeed the color of the roof in the background of the actual image, and generated images tend to be poorly formed when the object layout or background is complex, as depicted by the second, third and seventh rows of Fig. <a href="#S4.F5" title="Fig. 5 ‣ IV-B Comparative Results Targeting ResNet-50 ‣ IV Experimental Results ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. We hypothesis that the quality of generated images may be linked to the performance of the target feature extractor and examine this point in the next experiment using ViT that is more accurate than ResNet-50.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">IV-C</span> </span><span id="S4.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Results Targeting ViT-H14/L</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Fig. <a href="#S4.F7" title="Fig. 7 ‣ IV-C Results Targeting ViT-H14/L ‣ IV Experimental Results ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> shows images generated by our decoder when using ViT as the target feature extractor. In particular, ViT-H/14 configured with ViT_H_14_ Weights.IMAGENET1K_SWAG_E2E_V1 <span id="footnote7" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">7</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">7</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">7</span>
              
              
              
            <a href="https://docs.pytorch.org/vision/main/models/generated/torchvision.models.vit_h_14.html#torchvision.models.ViT_H_14_Weights" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://docs.pytorch.org/vision/main/models/generated/torchvision.models.vit_h_14.html#torchvision.models.ViT_H_14_Weights</a></span></span></span> is used because it is ranked at the top in the ranking of the pre-trained models in Pytorch <span id="footnote8" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">8</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">8</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">8</span>
              
              
              
            <a href="https://docs.pytorch.org/vision/main/models.html#table-of-all-available-classification-weights" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://docs.pytorch.org/vision/main/models.html#table-of-all-available-classification-weights</a> (accessed on July 30, 2025)</span></span></span>. In this ranking, ResNet-50 used in the previous section achieves a top-1 accuracy of <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="77.374" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>77.374</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">77.374</annotation></semantics></math>% on ImageNet-1K classification whereas ViT-H/14 reaches <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="88.552" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>88.552</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">88.552</annotation></semantics></math>%. The experiment below aims to examine whether this performance gap is related to images that our decoder generates by targeting ResNet-50 and ViT-H/14. Similar to the experiments before, in Fig. <a href="#S4.F7" title="Fig. 7 ‣ IV-C Results Targeting ViT-H14/L ‣ IV Experimental Results ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{f}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{f}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> is extracted by ViT-H/14 from each actual image and the best images obtained by running our decoder three times are displayed next to it. In terms of ViT-H/14 features, the average pairwise squared Euclidean distance among the <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>15</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">15</annotation></semantics></math> actual images and the one among the <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math> carefully-selected tusker images from ImageNet dataset (please see Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A 10 Carefully-chosen Tusker Images ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> for these images) are <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2914.379" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2914.379</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2914.379</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="715.504" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>715.504</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">715.504</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. By taking these values as a reference, it can be said that our decoder successfully generates images whose features are remarkably similar to <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{f}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{f}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F7" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.07936v1/figure_gen_result_vit2_s.png" id="S4.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:429/503;" width="429" height="503" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 7: </span>Images generated by our decoder when using ViT-H/14 as the target feature extractor. For each actual image, our decoder is run three times and the best image in each run is shown with the squared Euclidean distance between its feature and the one of the actual image.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">By comparing images generated by targeting ResNet-50 in Figs. <a href="#S4.F5" title="Fig. 5 ‣ IV-B Comparative Results Targeting ResNet-50 ‣ IV Experimental Results ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> and <a href="#S4.F6" title="Fig. 6 ‣ IV-B Comparative Results Targeting ResNet-50 ‣ IV Experimental Results ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> to the ones generated by targeting ViT-H/14 in Figs. <a href="#S4.F7" title="Fig. 7 ‣ IV-C Results Targeting ViT-H14/L ‣ IV Experimental Results ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, the latter clearly preserve more detailed contents in the corresponding actual images than the former. For example, the top row on the left side of Fig. <a href="#S4.F7" title="Fig. 7 ‣ IV-C Results Targeting ViT-H14/L ‣ IV Experimental Results ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> demonstrates that the dogs in the generated images have objects around their necks (although one of them is red), whereas such objects are absent in the generated images in the top row of Fig. <a href="#S4.F5" title="Fig. 5 ‣ IV-B Comparative Results Targeting ResNet-50 ‣ IV Experimental Results ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. In addition, the second row from the bottom on the left side of Fig. <a href="#S4.F7" title="Fig. 7 ‣ IV-C Results Targeting ViT-H14/L ‣ IV Experimental Results ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> shows that the pizzas in the generated images and the actual one look the same. Moreover, as seen from the bottom row on the right side of Fig. <a href="#S4.F7" title="Fig. 7 ‣ IV-C Results Targeting ViT-H14/L ‣ IV Experimental Results ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, both of the generated images and the actual one contain tall buildings in the background (although they appear to be drawn in the sky in the left generated image). Thus, the preservation of detailed contents in images generated by our decoder can be thought to indicate the usefulness of features extracted by the target feature extractor. This corresponds to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>’s conclusion that features (logit vectors) extracted by a better image classification model retains richer information.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Regarding the comparison between CLIP’s image encoder with ResNet-50 backbone in Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="IV-A Results Targeting CLIP’s Image Encoder ‣ IV Experimental Results ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV-A</span></a> and ViT-H/14 in this section, the latter is expected to outperform the former on ImageNet-1K classification. The reason is that even if a more advanced CLIP’s image encoder with ViT-Large/14 backbone is carefully finetuned on ImageNet-1K dataset, its top-1 accuracy is <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="88.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>88.0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">88.0</annotation></semantics></math>% <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite>, which is lower than the one (<math id="S4.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="88.552" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>88.552</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">88.552</annotation></semantics></math>%) of ViT-H/14 in this section. Correspondingly, the generated images in Fig. <a href="#S4.F7" title="Fig. 7 ‣ IV-C Results Targeting ViT-H14/L ‣ IV Experimental Results ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> seem better than the ones in Figs. <a href="#S4.F3" title="Fig. 3 ‣ IV-A Results Targeting CLIP’s Image Encoder ‣ IV Experimental Results ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and <a href="#S4.F4" title="Fig. 4 ‣ IV-A Results Targeting CLIP’s Image Encoder ‣ IV Experimental Results ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> in terms of the preservation of detailed contents, as seen by comparing birds and foods in these images. However, the main advantage of CLIP’s image encoder is its zero-shot performance, that is, the generality of features extracted by it. As represented by the six actual images from the bottom on the right side of Fig. <a href="#S4.F7" title="Fig. 7 ‣ IV-C Results Targeting ViT-H14/L ‣ IV Experimental Results ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, most of images in ImageNet-1K dataset prominently display the main object near the centre. Thus, when targeting ViT-H/14 trained on such images, generated images tend to spend a large region for the main object and lose its surrounding context. In contrast, as can be seen from Figs. <a href="#S4.F3" title="Fig. 3 ‣ IV-A Results Targeting CLIP’s Image Encoder ‣ IV Experimental Results ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and <a href="#S4.F4" title="Fig. 4 ‣ IV-A Results Targeting CLIP’s Image Encoder ‣ IV Experimental Results ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, such a surrounding context is better preserved in the images generated by targeting CLIP’s image encoder. This context preservation could be one indicator of the generality of features extracted by the target feature extractor.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS4.6" class="ltx_text">IV-D</span> </span><span id="S4.SS4.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Discussions</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section presents four issues observed from the experiments before and discuss research topics that warrant further investigation. In particular, the first two issues are for the improvement of our decoder and the remaining two are for the construction of a better feature space.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS4.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S4.SS4.SSS1.6" class="ltx_text">IV-D</span>1 </span>Enhancing the loss function of our decoder</h4>

<div id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">It can be hypothesised that if we could improve our decoder to generate more realistic and natural images, those images would be located in closer proximity to the corresponding actual image in the feature space, compared images generated in this paper. As observed in images generated by targeting ViT-H/14 in Fig. <a href="#S4.F7" title="Fig. 7 ‣ IV-C Results Targeting ViT-H14/L ‣ IV Experimental Results ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, one typical artifact making generated images unrealistic and unnatural is large black regions, resulting from excessive modification of the predicted source noise <math id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\theta}\left(\boldsymbol{z}_{t},t\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\theta}\left(\boldsymbol{z}_{t},t\right)</annotation></semantics></math> in Eq. (<a href="#S3.E3" title="In III-A Overview of Our Decoder ‣ III Our Guided-diffusion-model-based Decoder for Feature Space Analysis ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) (Appendix <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Effect of ∇_{𝑡⁢ℎ⁢𝑟⁢𝑒⁢𝑠} ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a> presents how gradient clipping mitigates this artifact). When a large region in an image is spent for such artifacts, only the remaining region can be used to make its feature match a user-specified one, which significantly limits the possibility of images that can be generated. One promising solution is to enhance the loss function by adopting a perceptual quality assessment of a generated image <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>, <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite> in addition to the squared Euclidean distance between its feature and the user-specified one. This reduces artifact creation and enables our decoder to use the entire image region, increasing the possibility of a close feature match.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S4.SS4.SSS2.6" class="ltx_text">IV-D</span>2 </span>Recuding the computation time of our decoder</h4>

<div id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">One drawback of our decoder is its computation time. Specifically, NVIDIA GeForce RTX 3090 was used to generate the images in Figs. <a href="#S4.F3" title="Fig. 3 ‣ IV-A Results Targeting CLIP’s Image Encoder ‣ IV Experimental Results ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and <a href="#S4.F4" title="Fig. 4 ‣ IV-A Results Targeting CLIP’s Image Encoder ‣ IV Experimental Results ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> by targeting CLIP’s image encoder with ResNet-50 backbone and the average computation time per image generation run is <math id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8830" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>8830</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8830</annotation></semantics></math> seconds. Using the same GPU, the images in Figs. <a href="#S4.F5" title="Fig. 5 ‣ IV-B Comparative Results Targeting ResNet-50 ‣ IV Experimental Results ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> and <a href="#S4.F6" title="Fig. 6 ‣ IV-B Comparative Results Targeting ResNet-50 ‣ IV Experimental Results ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> were generated by targeting ResNet-50 with an average computation time of <math id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8951" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>8951</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8951</annotation></semantics></math> seconds per run. The images generated by targeting ViT-H/14 in Fig. <a href="#S4.F7" title="Fig. 7 ‣ IV-C Results Targeting ViT-H14/L ‣ IV Experimental Results ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> were generated using NVIDIA GeForce RTX 3090 and NVIDIA RTX 4000 Ada, which required on average <math id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="16897" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>16897</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16897</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="22694" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>22694</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">22694</annotation></semantics></math> seconds per run, respectively. One solution for reducing these computation times <span id="footnote9" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">9</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">9</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">9</span>
                
                
                
              Our decoder consumes about <math id="footnote9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="15.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>15.1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">15.1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="footnote9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="14.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>14.9</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">14.9</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="footnote9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="19.6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>19.6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">19.6</annotation></semantics></math>GB of VRAM for image generation runs targeting CLIP’s image encoder with ResNet-50 backbone, ResNet-50 and ViT-H/14, respectively.</span></span></span> is to implement our decoder on a fast sampler that can be incorporated into a pre-trained diffusion model to generate images with a small number of steps <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>, <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S4.SS4.SSS3.6" class="ltx_text">IV-D</span>3 </span>Smooth alignment between features and visual appearances</h4>

<div id="S4.SS4.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">One main finding from the experiments before is that unrealistic and unnatural images (generated by our decoder) are located in close proximity to an actual image in the feature space. Although these generated images capture the main characteristics of the actual image, they exhibit limited visual similarity. This suggests a potential direction for improving current feature extractors, that is, it may be possible to design a training objective that enforces a smooth transition in visual appearances of images according to their Euclidean distances in the feature space.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S4.SS4.SSS4.6" class="ltx_text">IV-D</span>4 </span>Suitability of CLIP’s multimodal feature space</h4>

<div id="S4.SS4.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Since CLIP is a visual-semantic embedding model that can directly measure similarities between images and textual captions by projecting them into a common feature (embedding) space, one interesting question is what kind of image has the same feature to the one of a caption. To check this, our decoder is run to generate an image whose feature closely matches the one of a caption. As shown in the columns named “Caption” and “Actual” in Fig. <a href="#S4.F8" title="Fig. 8 ‣ IV-D4 Suitability of CLIP’s multimodal feature space ‣ IV-D Discussions ‣ IV Experimental Results ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, we use captions that are assigned to the actual images in MSCOCO dataset. In other words, we aim to examine whether an image generated by defining <math id="S4.SS4.SSS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{f}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{f}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> as the feature of a caption is similar to the corresponding actual image. As seen from the column named “Original”, generated images are completely different from their corresponding actual images, and their features exhibit huge squared Euclidean distances to those of the corresponding captions.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F8" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.07936v1/figure_gen_result_t2i_s.png" id="S4.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/581;" width="476" height="581" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 8: </span>Images generated by our decoder when using CLIP’s image encoder with ResNet-50 backbone as the target feature extractor. For the second and third columns, <math id="S4.F8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{f}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{f}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as the feature of each caption and its normalised one, respectively. On the other hand, <math id="S4.F8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{f}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{f}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> is set to <math id="S4.F8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1.2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.F8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.8</annotation></semantics></math> times the feature of each actual image. The number under each generated image indicates the squared Euclidean distance between its feature and <math id="S4.F8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{f}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{f}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> used to generated it.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS4.SSS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let us investigate the reason for the aforementioned result. First, CLIP is trained so that an image and the caption assigned to it are close in the feature space with respect to their (normalised) cosine similarity, not their squared Euclidean distance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>. Thus, despite their directional similarity, they are located in completely different regions in the feature space, as verified by the fact that the average norm of the features of the six actual images in Fig. <a href="#S4.F8" title="Fig. 8 ‣ IV-D4 Suitability of CLIP’s multimodal feature space ‣ IV-D Discussions ‣ IV Experimental Results ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> and the one of the corresponding six captions are <math id="S4.SS4.SSS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.177" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2.177</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.177</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS4.SSS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9.795" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>9.795</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9.795</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. This motivates us to run our decoder to generate an image by defining <math id="S4.SS4.SSS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{f}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{f}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> as the caption’s normalised feature whose norm is equal to the average feature norm of the six actual images. Nonetheless, the column named “Normalised” in Fig. <a href="#S4.F8" title="Fig. 8 ‣ IV-D4 Suitability of CLIP’s multimodal feature space ‣ IV-D Discussions ‣ IV Experimental Results ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> shows that the generated images significantly differ from the corresponding actual images, although they slightly align with the captions as typically observed in the second image from the bottom.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.SSS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The above failure of generating reasonable images is not due to our decoder. The columns named “<math id="S4.SS4.SSS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times 1.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>1.2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times 1.2</annotation></semantics></math>” and “<math id="S4.SS4.SSS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times 0.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>0.8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times 0.8</annotation></semantics></math>” present images generated by setting <math id="S4.SS4.SSS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{f}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{f}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS4.SSS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1.2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS4.SSS4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.8</annotation></semantics></math> times the feature of the corresponding actual image, respectively. The hypothesis for this is that the generated images and the actual one have the same normalised cosine similarity to the caption in terms of their features and should be semantically relevant to the caption. The images generated by our decoder appear to support this hypothesis. Thus, as long as there exist real images in proximity to a user-specified feature, our decoder can generate reasonable images capturing the main contents in those images. Therefore, we conclude that the huge Euclidean distance between the feature of each caption and that of the generated image suggests no existence of real images having this caption’s feature. Although the Euclidean distance could be reduced to some extent by changing the backbone diffusion model of our decoder from stable diffusion to a more advanced one, our conclusion is likely to remain valid. Like this, images and captions are located in completely different regions of CLIP’s feature space <span id="footnote10" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">10</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">10</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">10</span>
                
                
                
              This can be thought as the reason why the CLIP-based text-to-image in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> first converts the feature of a text prompt into the corresponding image feature.</span></span></span> although relevant image-caption pairs have relatively high angular similarities. It would be an important research topic to examine the suitability of such a multimodal feature space where images are only weakly associated with relevant captions.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">V </span><span id="S5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Conclusion and Future Work</span></h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This paper introduced a decoder that generates images whose features are ensured to closely match user-specified ones. Owing to the short Euclidean distances between these features, we can perform rigorous analysis of the feature space of a target feature extractor. Our decoder is implemented as a guided diffusion model that needs no training, instead guides a pre-trained diffusion model (stable diffusion in this paper) to minimise the squared Euclidean distance between the feature of a generated image and the user-specified one. Furthermore, the early step emphasis strategy and gradient normalisation and clipping are devised to improve our decoder’s image generation. The experiments using each of CLIP’s image encoder with ResNet-50 backbone, ResNet-50 and ViT-H/14 as the target feature extractor demonstrate that images generated by our decoder have features remarkably similar to the specified ones and offer valuable insights into the feature space of the extractor.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In addition to the four issues discussed in Section <a href="#S4.SS4" title="IV-D Discussions ‣ IV Experimental Results ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV-D</span></a>, we plan to implement our decoder on pre-trained diffusion models other than stable diffusion and to analyse feature spaces of more recent feature extractors than the ones employed in this paper (e.g., image encoders implemented in OpenCLIP project <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite>). Also, we aim to improve our decoder’s image generation by formulating the guidance-based reverse process as a search problem to find the optimal sequence of denoising operations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>, <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite>. Finally, our encoder can be readily extended to other modalities like text, audio and time-series to analyse their respective feature spaces.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span><math id="A1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math> Carefully-chosen Tusker Images</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Fig. <a href="#A1.F9" title="Fig. 9 ‣ Appendix A 10 Carefully-chosen Tusker Images ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> shows the <math id="A1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math> images used to compute the average squared Euclidean distance, which can be a reference to interpret how close the feature of an image generated by our decoder is to a user-specified feature.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.F9" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.07936v1/figure_tusker_images_s.png" id="A1.F9.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/146;" width="476" height="146" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 9: </span><math id="A1.F9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math> images that are carefully chosen from the tusker category of ImageNet dataset and validated by human to exhibit similar visual perception.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Effect of <math id="A2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{g}</annotation></semantics></math></h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To check the effect of <math id="A2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> in Eq. (<a href="#S3.E3" title="In III-A Overview of Our Decoder ‣ III Our Guided-diffusion-model-based Decoder for Feature Space Analysis ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), this experiment aims to investigate a transition of squared Euclidean distances between features of generated images and the ones of their corresponding actual images on <math id="A2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> values of <math id="A2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.5</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8</annotation></semantics></math>. Specifically, using CLIP’s image encoder with ResNet-50 backbone as the target feature extractor, <math id="A2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{f}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{f}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as the feature of each of the <math id="A2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>15</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">15</annotation></semantics></math> actual images in Figs. <a href="#S4.F3" title="Fig. 3 ‣ IV-A Results Targeting CLIP’s Image Encoder ‣ IV Experimental Results ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and <a href="#S4.F4" title="Fig. 4 ‣ IV-A Results Targeting CLIP’s Image Encoder ‣ IV Experimental Results ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. Except <math id="A2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>, all the other hyper-parameters are fixed as configured in Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="III-B Details of Our Decoder ‣ III Our Guided-diffusion-model-based Decoder for Feature Space Analysis ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III-B</span></a>. Similar to the experiments before, this experiment targets the best image whose feature yields the shortest squared Euclidean distance to <math id="A2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{f}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{f}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> in the image generation process.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Fig. <a href="#A2.F10" title="Fig. 10 ‣ Appendix B Effect of 𝑤_𝑔 ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> depicts a box plot that summarises the median, <math id="A2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="25" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>25</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">25</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="75" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>75</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">75</annotation></semantics></math> percentiles, and the maximum and minimum of squared Euclidean distances between the features of the <math id="A2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>15</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">15</annotation></semantics></math> best images generated by each <math id="A2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> value and the ones of the corresponding actual images. As seen from Fig. <a href="#A2.F10" title="Fig. 10 ‣ Appendix B Effect of 𝑤_𝑔 ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>, <math id="A2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{g}=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{g}=8</annotation></semantics></math> leads to images whose features exhibit shorter squared Euclidean distances than <math id="A2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{g}=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{g}=4</annotation></semantics></math> used in Section <a href="#S4" title="IV Experimental Results ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV</span></a>. Regarding this, Figs. <a href="#A2.F11" title="Fig. 11 ‣ Appendix B Effect of 𝑤_𝑔 ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> and <a href="#A2.F12" title="Fig. 12 ‣ Appendix B Effect of 𝑤_𝑔 ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a> presents the images generated by each <math id="A2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> value. As can be seen from the top row and the second row from the bottom of Fig. <a href="#A2.F11" title="Fig. 11 ‣ Appendix B Effect of 𝑤_𝑔 ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> and the second row from the bottom of Fig. <a href="#A2.F12" title="Fig. 12 ‣ Appendix B Effect of 𝑤_𝑔 ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>, <math id="A2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{g}=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{g}=8</annotation></semantics></math> sometimes results in too unrealistic images despite the short squared Euclidean distances. This can hinder the feature space analysis by visually checking generated images. Thus, <math id="A2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{g}=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{g}=4</annotation></semantics></math> is adopted to balance between minimising squared Euclidean distances and maintaining the realness of generated images.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.F10" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.07936v1/figure_wg_exp.svg" id="A2.F10.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/227;" width="476" height="227"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 10: </span>A box-plot of squared Euclidean distances between features of generated images and the ones of their corresponding actual images on different <math id="A2.F10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> values when using CLIP’s image encoder with ResNet-50 backbone as the target feature extractor.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A2.F11" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.07936v1/figure_wp_exp_images_s.png" id="A2.F11.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_portrait" style="aspect-ratio:381/615;" width="381" height="615" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 11: </span>Images generated by our decoder with <math id="A2.F11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> values of <math id="A2.F11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.5</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.F11.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.F11.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.F11.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.F11.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8</annotation></semantics></math> when using CLIP’s image encoder with ResNet-50 backbone as the target feature extractor. The best image in each image generation run is shown with the squared Euclidean distance between its feature and the one of the corresponding actual image.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A2.F12" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.07936v1/figure_wp_exp_images2_s.png" id="A2.F12.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_portrait" style="aspect-ratio:381/538;" width="381" height="538" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 12: </span>(Continued from Fig. <a href="#A2.F12" title="Fig. 12 ‣ Appendix B Effect of 𝑤_𝑔 ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>) Images generated by our decoder with <math id="A2.F12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> values of <math id="A2.F12.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.5</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.F12.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.F12.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.F12.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.F12.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8</annotation></semantics></math> when using CLIP’s image encoder with ResNet-50 backbone as the target feature extractor..</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Effect of the Early Step Emphasis Strategy</h2>

<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Using <math id="A3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{g}=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{g}=4</annotation></semantics></math> in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Effect of 𝑤_𝑔 ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>, an ablation study is conducted to compare image generation approaches with and without the early step emphasis strategy. That is, the former uses <math id="A3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=1000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=1000</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=8</annotation></semantics></math> self-recurrence iterations at the first <math id="A3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}=5</annotation></semantics></math> reverse steps and the others, respectively, whereas the latter uses <math id="A3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=8</annotation></semantics></math> at all steps. When using the early step emphasis strategy, the average squared Euclidean distance between the features of the <math id="A3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>15</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">15</annotation></semantics></math> generated images in Figs. <a href="#A2.F11" title="Fig. 11 ‣ Appendix B Effect of 𝑤_𝑔 ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> and <a href="#A2.F12" title="Fig. 12 ‣ Appendix B Effect of 𝑤_𝑔 ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a> and the ones of the corresponding actual images is <math id="A3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.06554\pm 0.03468" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.06554</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.03468</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.06554\pm 0.03468</annotation></semantics></math>. In contrast, without this strategy, the average is <math id="A3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.06906\pm 0.03185" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.06906</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.03185</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.06906\pm 0.03185</annotation></semantics></math>. This verifies the effectiveness of the early step emphasis strategy for generating images whose features match user-specified ones more closely.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>Effect of <math id="A4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{thres}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mo>∇</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{thres}</annotation></semantics></math></h2>

<div id="A4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To demonstrate <math id="A4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{thres}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mo>∇</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{thres}</annotation></semantics></math>’s effect on our decoder’s image generation, Fig. <a href="#A4.F13" title="Fig. 13 ‣ Appendix D Effect of ∇_{𝑡⁢ℎ⁢𝑟⁢𝑒⁢𝑠} ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a> shows images generated by setting <math id="A4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{thres}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mo>∇</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{thres}</annotation></semantics></math> to three times the standard deviation of values in <math id="A4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\boldsymbol{z}_{t}}l\left(f\left(\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}\right),\boldsymbol{f}_{s}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\boldsymbol{z}_{t}}l\left(f\left(\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}\right),\boldsymbol{f}_{s}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> when using ViT-H/14 as the target feature extractor. As seen from Fig. <a href="#A4.F13" title="Fig. 13 ‣ Appendix D Effect of ∇_{𝑡⁢ℎ⁢𝑟⁢𝑒⁢𝑠} ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>, a generated image often contains an artifactual (unintended) large black region that is caused by Eq. (<a href="#S3.E3" title="In III-A Overview of Our Decoder ‣ III Our Guided-diffusion-model-based Decoder for Feature Space Analysis ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) where the predicted source noise <math id="A4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\theta}\left(\boldsymbol{z}_{t},t\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\theta}\left(\boldsymbol{z}_{t},t\right)</annotation></semantics></math> is excessively modified with large gradient values in <math id="A4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\boldsymbol{z}_{t}}l\left(f\left(\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}\right),\boldsymbol{f}_{s}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\boldsymbol{z}_{t}}l\left(f\left(\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}\right),\boldsymbol{f}_{s}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>. The maximum gradient value used in this modification is reduced using a smaller <math id="A4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{thres}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mo>∇</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{thres}</annotation></semantics></math> like twice the standard deviation of values in <math id="A4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\boldsymbol{z}_{t}}l\left(f\left(\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}\right),\boldsymbol{f}_{s}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\boldsymbol{z}_{t}}l\left(f\left(\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}\right),\boldsymbol{f}_{s}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, as is the case for Fig. <a href="#S4.F7" title="Fig. 7 ‣ IV-C Results Targeting ViT-H14/L ‣ IV Experimental Results ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>. As a result, a generated image becomes less likely to contain an artifactual large black region, although several images in Fig. <a href="#S4.F7" title="Fig. 7 ‣ IV-C Results Targeting ViT-H14/L ‣ IV Experimental Results ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> still have such regions. In addition, setting <math id="A4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{thres}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mo>∇</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{thres}</annotation></semantics></math> to a further lower value prevents our decoder from generating images whose features are sufficiently close to a user-specified feature. One possible solution for artifactual large black regions is discussed in Section <a href="#S4.SS4" title="IV-D Discussions ‣ IV Experimental Results ‣ Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV-D</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A4.F13" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.07936v1/figure_grad_clip_3_vit_s.png" id="A4.F13.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/237;" width="476" height="237" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 13: </span>Example images generated by our decoder when using ViT-H/14 as the target feature extractor and set <math id="A4.F13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{thres}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mo>∇</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{thres}</annotation></semantics></math> to three times the standard deviation of values in <math id="A4.F13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\boldsymbol{z}_{t}}l\left(f\left(\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}\right),\boldsymbol{f}_{s}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\boldsymbol{z}_{t}}l\left(f\left(\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}\right),\boldsymbol{f}_{s}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
